Norwegian law does not regulate hitchhiking (''haiking''); it regulates who may be on the motorway. [https://lovdata.no/dokument/SF/forskrift/1986-03-21-747 § 4(2) of the ''trafikkreglene''] permits only motor vehicles that may lawfully do at least 40 km/h on a ''motorveg'' or ''motortrafikkveg'' — and, unusually explicitly, on the entry and exit roads to them as well, so the on-ramp is out too, not just the carriageway and the shoulder. § 17(1)(e) forbids stopping anywhere on those roads and their ramps, and § 11(2) forbids reversing and turning, so a driver who has passed you cannot legally come back for you. That leaves the signposted ''rasteplasser'' and petrol stations, where you ask people face to face — the normal way to travel the Norwegian motorway network. Everywhere else hitchhiking is legal; stand on the verge where a driver can see you and pull over safely.
